Once you sustain $5-10m net worth, you've made it. Almost. Rich enough to be comfortable, poor enough for one bad bet to send you back. Here's what actually changes at this tier, and how to get to $25m 👇

-Self-funding, barely. A 3% draw on $7.5m is $225k/yr pre-tax, ~$150k after. You can quit but probably shouldn't. Earned income is still the cheapest capital you'll ever get plus it’s more reliable. -Risk capacity is real but finite. A venture bet going to zero is a bad year. Size so the worst case costs you a year or two, not a tier demotion. -Borrow, carefully. SBLOCs work here, just at worse spreads than the private bank crowd gets. Keep LTV under 20% so a drawdown doesn’t become a margin call. -Skip the fancy structures. Estate exemption is $15m per person. You don't have an estate tax problem, you have an income tax problem. QSBS, solo 401k, tax-loss harvesting, no state tax residency or Puerto Rico if extreme/mobile. Boring, cheap, compounding. -Claude Max is 0.03% of your net worth. Fading the singularity at this tier is a choice.

How to get to the next tier: 👇

-Index funds at 10% take ~13 years to turn $7.5m into $25m. Nobody gets there on VTI alone. -You need one more engine: a business, carry, equity in something, or one asymmetric bet. Concentration got you here, diversification keeps you here, sized concentration gets you out. -Never round-trip. The #1 killer at this tier isn't missing the next 10x, it's giving back the last one. Take chips off on the way up. -Don't buy in tier1/2 location. You can have a sick house in the Midwest or rent. -Keep 2-3 years of spend in T-bills so you never sell the good stuff at the bottom.

Ancillary: -Business class optional. Be price-sensitive and value-discerning. Is it worth it? -Elite bedding yes, private chef no. Learn to cook. -Care less, but not entirely. Someone in the replies calls you mid. At $7m you still notice. At $100m you won't. -Crypto is probably how you got here. Take some of it off the table. ETFs, not a seed phrase in a sock drawer. -Nanny? Wait to have kids. Nanny/Night nurse is a $25m line item.

Follow, like, RT, and comment if you're stuck in mid-high 7fig Hell and want to get unstuck.

